{
  "format": "waddle.behavior/v0",
  "name": "bimanual_part_scoped_gripper_crosses_anchorless_blend",
  "description": "Pins docs/FSM.md §5's ONE exception to the anchorless part-scoped hold (flags waddle.v0.agent + waddle.v0.parts). In an agent-invited episode the caller's ticks are Noop{NOOP_REASON_AGENT_EPISODE} (E24), so when the invited agent's claim engages under IMMEDIATE{blendNs:300000000} the gate has NO last commanded point to fade from. The two shapes then part company, and this scenario scores the difference in one window: a part-scoped ARM row has no endpoint at all — anchoring it on itself would fade one arm's command in as if it commanded the whole robot — so it holds, consumed and dropped like every other §5 hold; a part-scoped GRIPPER-ONLY action commands one scalar and fabricates no arm row for anyone, so with nothing to fade from it is its own endpoint whether or not it names a part, and crosses the window unchanged, still tagged 'left'. Narrowing the exception to 'no anchor and no tag' would silently drop a commanded grip in exactly the episodes that have no anchor — an agent-invited one, this one. Neither is faulted: both fit the declared space and were accepted at the intake (contrast bimanual_part_dims_mismatch_faults). The closing step shows the window ending: past blendNs the next part-scoped arm row substitutes normally, part-tagged. Judgment calls: exactly one caller tick before engage, which must NOT establish an anchor (that is the whole premise, and expect_output pins it as a noop); the gripper rides a NoopMarker target, which is how control.proto spells 'hold the arm, move the gripper' and what flatten_action builds from a part-scoped noop plus a gripper; the post-window tick is at t=550ms, comfortably past the 200-500ms window, since the exact boundary tick is deliberately unasserted here as in handoff_immediate_mid_chunk.",
